    Compiler: the union-splitting cost in a test of `callsig_splits`; two comments
    
    `unionsplitcost` leaves the largest union out of the product it counts, so a signature with one union
    of five members is within the cost, and the test that expected no splits for it failed. The test now
    takes two unions of three members each, whose cost (six) is past the default of four.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/Compiler/test/inference.jl b/Compiler/test/inference.jl
index 97d40fd585..c536f0b1c2 100644
--- a/Compiler/test/inference.jl
+++ b/Compiler/test/inference.jl
@@ -6743,6 +6743,6 @@ let sigs = Compiler.callsig_splits(Tuple{typeof(!==), Union{Nothing,Int}, Nothin
     @test sigs !== nothing && Set(sigs) == Set(Any[Tuple{typeof(!==), Nothing, Nothing}, Tuple{typeof(!==), Int, Nothing}])
 end
 @test Compiler.callsig_splits(Tuple{typeof(!==), Int, Nothing}) === nothing
-@test Compiler.callsig_splits(Tuple{typeof(!==), Union{Int8,Int16,Int32,Int64,Int128}, Nothing}) === nothing
+@test Compiler.callsig_splits(Tuple{typeof(!==), Union{Int8,Int16,Int32}, Union{Nothing,Missing,Float64}}) === nothing
 
 end # module inference
